-- §7b  How many remainders the arithmetic can reach at all, which is §7's denominator.
#
# ONE ROW, ONE NUMBER: the layers where `r = n - d` is computable, because both operands are
# stated. layers/remainder.sqlc inner-joins demand to nameplate, so its cardinality IS the
# answer and nothing further is filtered here.
#
# ⭐⭐ IT EXISTS SO THE EXAMPLE STOPS REACHING PAST TWO RELATIONS INTO `pm.layer` AND
#    `pm.nameplate`. The count was written inline in Rust as a join with the two
#    `IS NOT NULL` predicates spelled out again -- the same two predicates layers/demand.sqlc
#    and layers/nameplate.sqlc already own. Two copies of one population, and the day either
#    wrapper narrows, the example keeps passing while measuring a different set.
#
# ⛔ THE ASSERTION IT FEEDS IS A SECOND WITNESS, NOT THE FIRST. algebra/remainder_standing.sqlc
#   asserts the same equality by counting layers/remainder.sqlc against
#   layers/remainder_scope.sqlc, and examples/soundness.rs runs it. This route reaches the same
#   number through the example that prints the standings, which is the point of having two.
-- layers/remainder.sqlc, counted.
SELECT count(*) AS "computable!"
FROM (
    :compose(layers/remainder.sqlc)
) r
